digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates and evaluates INO-3107, a DNA immunotherapy encoding HPV-6/11 antigens with an IL-12 adjuvant, as a biologically motivated approach for recurrent respiratory papillomatosis that may elicit HPV-specific immune responses and reduce the need for repeated surgical intervention.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a chronic HPV-driven disease has substantial surgical burden, a plausible immune mechanism, a targeted DNA-immunotherapy design, and Phase 1/2 safety, efficacy, and immune-response evidence, the paper-level aim is to evaluate that immunotherapy as a treatment strategy."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) INO-3107 has biologically rational design features for RRP because it encodes HPV-6/11 antigens and includes an IL-12 immune-adjuvant component."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Clinical observations that immune-based interventions can affect RRP, together with evidence that an immune-therapeutic can enhance HPV-6/11-specific T-cell immunity, make immune modulation a plausible strategy to investigate in RRP."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the plausibility of immune modulation in RRP and the rationale for restoring HPV-specific cytotoxic immunity support investigating HPV-specific immunotherapy as a strategy to improve RRP control."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Given a supported rationale for HPV-specific immunotherapy and a clinical need to decrease RRP surgical frequency, clinical trial evaluation of such immunotherapy is motivated."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The unmet need in RRP and the biologically rational INO-3107 candidate together motivate Phase 1/2 evaluation of safety, efficacy, and immunogenicity."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The paper’s report of completed Phase 1/2 results aligns with the motivated clinical evaluation of INO-3107 for RRP."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) RRP can have clinically serious manifestations because severe lesions may impair breathing and rare malignant transformation is described as a severe complication."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) The Phase 1/2 trial provides evidence on INO-3107 safety, efficacy, and induced immune responses in the RRP trial context."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Across the disease burden, surgical limitations, immune rationale, INO-3107 design, and Phase 1/2 trial evidence, the paper is best understood as testing INO-3107 as an HPV-specific DNA immunotherapy for RRP."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) The combination of substantial RRP burden and the need to reduce harmful repeated surgery supports an unmet need for improved non-surgical or adjunct treatment approaches."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because repeated surgery is the mainstay yet cumulative surgical morbidity and injury risk are likely, surgery alone is an inadequate long-term solution and adjuvant medical therapies should be pursued."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) In RRP, evidence of inadequate HPV-specific cellular immunity supports the rationale that restoring HPV-specific cytotoxic immunity could improve disease control."];
  "n_c_burden" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given RRP is chronic and debilitating and quality of life burden is high, therefore RRP represents a major clinical burden needing better therapy."];
  "n_c_design_targeted" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given a plausible platform exists and INO-3107 encodes HPV-6/11 antigens, therefore a construct encoding HPV-6/11 antigens (as INO-3107 does) is a rational candidate to test."];
  "n_c_dna_plausible" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given prior HPV DNA immunotherapies induce potent HPV immune responses and DNA-based vaccines can elicit cellular immune responses, therefore DNA immunotherapy is a plausible platform for therapeutic HPV immunity."];
  "n_c_dna_plausible2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given DNA immunotherapy is a plausible platform and DNA immunotherapy can elicit HPV-specific cellular immunity, therefore a DNA immunotherapy encoding relevant HPV antigens is a plausible approach for HPV-driven disease."];
  "n_c_il12_design" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given IL-12 can enhance cellular immunity and INO-3107 encodes IL-12, therefore including IL-12 in an HPV-antigen DNA immunotherapy is a rational design feature for RRP."];
  "n_c_immune_mechanism" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given papillomas show suppressive/regulatory immunity and persistent viral disease with weak cytotoxic clearance suggests persistence, therefore the best explanation is inadequate HPV-specific cellular immunity enabling persistence of HPV6/11 lesions."];
  "n_c_support_root" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the Phase 1/2 trial demonstrates safety and demonstrates efficacy, therefore the Phase 1/2 evaluation elements of safety and efficacy are substantiated in this trial context."];
  "n_c_surg_harm" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given repeated surgeries cause significant morbidity and injury risk increases with each surgery, therefore cumulative harm from surgical management is likely."];
  "n_c_surgery_need2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given cumulative surgical harm exists and surgery alone is inadequate long-term, therefore reducing surgery frequency is a key therapeutic goal."];
  "n_c_target" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given RRP is caused by HPV-6/11 and immunotherapy should encode antigens from the causal viral types, therefore HPV-6/11 antigens are appropriate therapeutic targets."];
  "n_i_best_expl" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple statements describe disease burden, limits of surgery, immune dysfunction, and a specific immunotherapy candidate/trial, then the best explanation is a proposal to develop/test that immunotherapy to improve RRP outcomes."];
  "n_i_combo_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If adding an immune adjuvant like IL-12 can enhance cellular immunity, then including IL-12 in an HPV-antigen DNA immunotherapy may strengthen therapeutic immune responses."];
  "n_i_design_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a platform is plausible and correct antigen targets are identified, then a construct encoding those targets is a rational candidate to test."];
  "n_i_immune_gap_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a persistent viral disease is associated with suppressed/regulatory local immunity and weak cytotoxic clearance, then an intervention that restores HPV-specific cytotoxic immunity could improve disease control."];
  "n_i_need" [label="(0,0,0) If a disease causes substantial morbidity/burden and standard care is high-risk or repetitive, then improved non-surgical or adjunct therapies are needed."];
  "n_i_platform_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If DNA immunotherapy can elicit HPV-specific cellular (cytotoxic/CD8+) immunity, then a DNA immunotherapy encoding the relevant HPV antigens is a plausible therapeutic approach for HPV-driven disease."];
  "n_i_report_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a manuscript reports results from a completed Phase 1/2 trial assessing safety, efficacy, and immune responses of a candidate, then the manuscript embodies the research proposal to perform that evaluation."];
  "n_i_surgery_limits" [label="(0,0,0) If repeated surgery is the mainstay yet increases morbidity and injury risk, then surgery alone is an inadequate long-term solution and adjuvant medical therapies should be pursued."];
  "n_i_target_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a disease is caused by specific viral types, then immunotherapy should encode antigens from those viral types to focus immune attack."];
  "n_i_trial_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If there is unmet need and a biologically justified candidate therapy, then a Phase 1/2 trial should evaluate safety, efficacy, and immunogenicity."];
  "n_r19_9" [label="(11,19,9) Currently, an immune-therapeutic can enhance HPV 6/11-specific T-cell immunity in RRP context."];
  "n_r3_8" [label="(2,3,8) Currently, RRP has a high burden on patient quality of life."];
  "n_r6_9" [label="(4,6,9) Currently, severe RRP complications can include malignant transformation (rare)."];
  "n_r8_17" [label="(7,8,17) Currently, there is a clinical need to identify medical therapies that decrease RRP surgical frequency."];
  "n_r9_26" [label="(9,9,26) Currently, DNA-based vaccines can elicit cellular immune responses."];
  "n_s10a" [label="(10,1,0) Currently, prior HPV DNA immunotherapies have induced potent HPV immune responses."];
  "n_s11b" [label="(11,2,0) Currently, those clinical results support that immune-based interventions can impact RRP disease state."];
  "n_s12a" [label="(12,1,0) Currently, INO-3107 is a DNA immunotherapy encoding antigens from both HPV-6 and HPV-11."];
  "n_s12b" [label="(12,2,0) Currently, INO-3107 also encodes an IL-12 immune adjuvant."];
  "n_s13c" [label="(13,3,0) Currently, the Phase 1/2 trial demonstrates safety of INO-3107."];
  "n_s13d" [label="(13,4,0) Currently, the Phase 1/2 trial demonstrates efficacy of INO-3107."];
  "n_s13e" [label="(13,5,0) Currently, the Phase 1/2 trial demonstrates immune responses induced by INO-3107."];
  "n_s2a" [label="(2,1,0) Currently, HPV-6 and HPV-11 are well-established causes of RRP."];
  "n_s3c" [label="(3,3,0) Currently, in severe cases RRP lesions can impair breathing."];
  "n_s5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, RRP is chronic, debilitating, and imposes substantial patient burden."];
  "n_s6b" [label="(6,2,0) Currently, repeated surgical removal can cause significant morbidity (multiple procedures, voice impact, anesthesia, psychological and financial burden)."];
  "n_s7" [label="(7,1,0) Currently, the risk of laryngeal injury increases with each additional RRP surgery."];
  "n_s9a" [label="(9,1,0) Currently, RRP papillomas exhibit suppressive/regulatory immune functions."];
  "n_s5" -> "n_c_burden" [label="induction-case"];
  "n_r3_8" -> "n_c_burden" [label="induction-common"];
